#---
# purpose: "a module's own get/2 is called, and bracket access still indexes"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # module_get_is_not_index
#
# `m[key]` is parsed as a call to `Access.get/2` and turned back into an index
# during decoding. The predicate that recognised it asked only whether the
# callee was SOME alias whose field was `get`, so every user-defined `get/2`
# was swallowed by the same rule:
#
#     defmodule Store do
#       def get(_map, _key), do: :from_store
#     end
#     Store.get(%{a: 1}, :a)    # was 1 -- the body never ran, no diagnostic
#
# Silent, and squarely in the way: `Map.get/2` and `Keyword.get/2` are both
# shaped exactly like that, and both are due in this epic.
#
# ## What it covers
#
#   * a module whose `get/2` shadows nothing and must simply be called;
#   * `get/3` beside it, which was never hijacked, so the two must not diverge;
#   * bracket access on a map, still indexing, including a missing key;
#   * an alias whose LOCAL NAME is `Access` -- `alias Store, as: Access` -- must
#     reach the aliased module, not the bracket sugar. This is the case that
#     shows why the callee cannot be recognised by its alias: decoding runs
#     before alias resolution, so the segments are identical either way. The
#     front door stamps a marker instead;
#   * a captured `&Store.get/2`, which is a `get` that is not a direct call.
#
# Note what is deliberately absent: `Access.get(m, :a)` written longhand. fz has
# no `Access` module, so that is now an unresolved call with a diagnostic. It
# used to "work" only because the sugar swallowed it, which is the bug this
# fixture exists for. See the Access-module ticket.
def main() do
  m = %{a: 1, b: 2}
  dbg({m[:a], m[:b], m[:missing]})

  dbg({Store.get(m, :a), Store.get(m, :missing)})
  dbg(Aliased.through_alias(m))
  capture = &Store.get/2
  dbg(capture.(m, :captured))
  dbg({Store.get(m, :a, :fallback), Store.get(m, :missing, :fallback)})

  dbg(Store.describe(m))
end

defmodule Aliased do
  alias Store, as: Access

  @spec through_alias(any) :: any
  def through_alias(m), do: {Access.get(m, :a), Access.tag(1)}
end

defmodule Aliased do
  alias Store, as: Access

  @spec through_alias(any) :: any
  def through_alias(m), do: {Access.get(m, :a), Access.tag(1)}
end

defmodule Store do
  @spec get(any, any) :: any
  def get(_map, key), do: {:store_two, key}

  @spec get(any, any, any) :: any
  def get(_map, key, default), do: {:store_three, key, default}

  @spec tag(any) :: any
  def tag(x), do: {:store_tag, x}

  @spec describe(any) :: any
  def describe(map) do
    # the module's own get/2 from inside the module, beside a real index
    {get(map, :a), map[:a]}
  end
end
